Demo chrome (kurtbruns/demo.getkestrel.dev#9, revised in #23)

What: Kestrel's dev-only "Open dashboard" pill, extended into a split pill in the same
bottom-right corner on every surface. Its inverted tag reads "Demo" and opens a short
note about the sandbox (a <details>, so it needs no script); the other half is the one
action that matters there. On every public page (card pages, the reader shell, a hosted
post page) that's "Open dashboard"; in the editor, "View publication", which the editor
and the docs room otherwise lack. In the editor the note also carries "Reset demo...", a
nested step whose Reset button posts to the demo Worker's /_demo/reset; Cancel, Escape
and a click outside close what's open. The pill is fixed, so it adds nothing to the
layout: it steps up over the save bar while it shows, and on a phone the toasts rise
above it. In the demo's auth mode ("demo", from 0001-demo-auth) the identity chip reads
"Demo sandbox" with no sign-out link. A client spec covers the editor side.

Why: a visitor should always know they're in a disposable sandbox, and how to start
over. #23 replaced #9's full-width strip, which sat at the bottom of the public pages
but the top of the editor and made the editor scroll (its height, --demo-h, came off
the layout). The public pill rides the dev pill's injection points (devDashboardStyle
and devDashboardBadge in src/lib/page.ts), which reach only pages as served, never a
frozen render or a sent email (I3); in this build those helpers always emit the demo
pill in the dev pill's place, so a future caller elsewhere would carry it too. Reset
lives in the editor only: resetting is a publisher action, and a public page could not
carry the form anyway (a post page forbids form posts, form-action 'none').

SPEC §11 says no public page links into the Access-gated admin; its one exception is the
dev-only shortcut. The pill's "Open dashboard" link departs from that on purpose: in
the demo the editor has no Access wall (0001-demo-auth), and each visitor's editor is
their own sandbox, so the reason for the rule doesn't hold. No I-invariant is touched.

Kestrel's own server specs that assert the dev pill's presence or absence
(test/archive.spec.ts) fail on the patched tree, as its auth specs do under 0001; its
client specs pass.

See DESIGN.md, "Static assets and demo chrome" and "The patch set".

